X-Reposting-Policy: redistribute only with permission

KMOSTA01@ulkyvx.louisville.edu says:
> 
> was not for the government monopoly of currency, market would decide again
> (as it once decided in favor of bimetalism -- not gold, bimetalism, 
> I suggest Milton Friedman's "Money Mischief" as a great reading on
> this).

Yes and no. There has been natural bimetalism and even trimetalism in
free banking environments, but generally it hasn't been that stable
over the long term, because the ratios of market value of the metals
have not been that stable. This has often resulted in nasty
problems. I'd suggest that gold makes a better Schelling point over
all. This is not to say that I don't agree that the market is the
final arbiter, or that other schemes might have advantages over gold.
I merely argue that people are used to it and it has reasonable
properties. It is, of course, entirely possible that I'm wrong on this
-- this isn't the sort of thing for which extremely hard evidence is
available. Luckily, it isn't really much of an issue -- the important
thing is free banking, not the underlying monetary standard.

