Date: Fri, 7 May 93 11:19:48 CDT
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: Nanotech Beanstalks?

I've been told I'm an expert on 'beanstalks', so I'll give it a go.
First of all, I prefer the term 'Orbital Suspension Bridge', as it
is much more explanatory.

Background:

All long objects in Earth orbit want to orient in an up-down
direction.  The reason is the Earth's gravity field is an
inverse square field, i.e. non-linear.  Consider a horizontal
rod.  All parts of the rod are equidistant from the Earth's
center, and hence have the same potential energy.  Now rotate
the rod vertical.  The part closer to the Earth now has a lower
potential and the part farther from the Earth has a higher 
potential.  Since the gravity field is non-linear, the change
in potential on the lower end is more than the change in
potential in the upper end.  The net result is that the vertical
rod has a lower total potential energy than the same rod 
horizontal.  This holds true of any object that is not spherically
symmetric.

Real objects in orbit can swing about the viertical position, but
if there are damping forces, they will end up stably vertical.
Now, imagine a vertical cable of some intermediate length, like
100 km, in low Earth orbit.  The center of mass of the cable
orbits the Earth at the usual rate.  The bottom end, however
is 50 km closer to the earth.  Normally, objects closer to the
earth move in faster orbits.  Since the cable is all connected,
the bottom end is forced to move at the same angular rate as the
center of mass (that means they both complete and orbit in the
same amount of time).  Thus the bottom end is constrained to
move a total distance less than the center of mass (the
path it follows has a smaller radius about the center of the
Earth, hench the velocity is lower).  But a free object would
move faster than the center of mass 50 km higher, so the
bottom end moves substantially slower than a free orbiting
object at that altitude.  For small cable lengths relative to
the radius of the Earth, the difference is 1.78 m/s per km
below the center of mass.

A special case of orbital suspension bridge is one where the
center of mass is at 35,000 km altitude.  In this case the
angular rate is the same as the rotation of the Earth, and
the bottom end of the cable would be at rest with respect
to the surface.  But any other length, longer or shorter,
is theoretically possible, and the altitude of the center of
mass can be anything you choose.

Real Structures

It can be calculated that an orbital suspension bridge that goes
from GEO to the ground requires a material the density of carbon
fiber and a tensile strength of 2,000,000 psi to be feasible.
This is well within the theoretical strength of carbon fiber.
Real fiber made in production quantities has a maximum strength
of 1,000,000 psi (Amoco T-1000).  Their technical opinion is that
fiber made using present techniques could be pushed to 1.2 million
psi if there was sufficient demand.  So we have a substantial
portion of the required strength.

Think about a suspension bridge that spans half of orbital
velocity, i.e. the bottom end moves at 3900 m/s with respect
to the ground.  This is feasible with today's materials.
Put a landing platform at the bottom end.  An SSTO flying to
that platform will have a payload fraction of 24% rather than
1.5% for an SSTO going to orbit by itself.  This is a pretty
big improvement.  In fact, for the first part of building
a suspension bridge in orbit, the payback time in increased
payload for an SSTO is about 7 flights. 

So, we can build these orbital structures today, and they would
make economic sense even at low flight rates like existing launch
vehicles experience.  In the future, when stronger materials are
available, you could upgrade your existing structure incrementally
(if you designed for this) and get even better performance.

Note that a real structure will be multi-stranded, due to the
risk of debris or meteorite impact.

Dani Eder

