Date: Fri, 7 May 1993 09:42:11 -0700 (PDT)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Fallibilism & Charles Peirce

D. Anton Sherwood:
> The lesson I hear in all
> this Korzybski talk is -- to quote Oliver Cromwell, of all people (and I am
> not told in what context he said it) -- "Bethink ye, by the bowels of
> Christ, that ye may be wrong." 

This is the main point of fallibilism, the philosophy of Charles Peirce,
which I'd like to thank Eric Raymond for pointing me to.  Skimming over
his work I've seen quite a bit that Korzybski later borrowed.
Peirce started out as a research chemist and physicist, and later went
into philosophy.  Peirce stresses the importance of the "irritation of
doubt", which frequently arises from actual or (in the case of
science) feigned indecision.  Once we have decided how we should
act, we have attained belief or habit (rule of action) which appeases
the irritation of doubt.  The results of action in turn may cause
further irritation of doubt, when results do not match beliefs.  

Peirce also points out that continual feigned indecision has
disadvantages compared to reaching beleifs via tradition, authority, 
etc. for some aspects of life (eg it is better suited to the scientists 
and philosopher than to the businessmen, for whom indecision is
often a handicap).  In this respect he is far
less dogmatic and more useful than Korzybski and others who
promote their methodologies as superior for everybody regardless
of their niche in life.

Peirce also discusses meaning in terms of habit. His paper titles
hint at what he covered: "How to Make our Ideas Clear", "The
Scientific Attitude and Fallibilism", "Logic as Semiotic: A
Theory of Signs", "On the Doctrine of Chances", "The Probability
of Induction", "The General Theory of Probable Inference", 
"The Essentials of Pragmatism", "Critical Common-Sensism",
"Perceptual Judgements", "The Architecture of Theories",
"Evolutionary Love", etc.  It reads like Korzybski stripped
of the repitition and moralistic opinionating about "sanity"
et. al.  As you can see, I've become quite enamoured of
fallibilism, and recommend Charles Peirce highly.

Nick Szabo					szabo@techbook.com

