Date: Fri, 7 May 93 13:27:41 EDT
From: Andrew S Hall <ashall@magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu>
Subject: List accounting

I am not necessarily opposed to some type of list accounting/reputation
market, but do wonder what effect it will have on spreading ideas.
If the primary purpose of the list is to debate technical matters among
the already converted, then the accounting procedures will faciliate
this. If the list is to be a method by which interested people who
may be less-informed about some specifics can learn, question, and become
part of the 'movement' (for want of a better word), then I am concerned
about the effect such accounting procedures will have.

If new people who are ignorant in some of the arcanities of what we discuss
are punished or even just discouraged, this would have a very bad effect
on the list. W/O new perspectives, new ideas, instead of a commons crisis
we would have an evolutionary dead-end.

Maybe an accounting procedure can include benefits for good questions
that cause high-quality threads. Since there is not yet an FAQ, some
very good questions may be redundant, but it would be wrong to punish
someone for that. (e.g. what about the effect of Heisenberg's Uncertainity
on uploading?) This type of accounting could very quickly select for
high quality questions while not punishing honest ignorance.

A.


