Date: Fri, 7 May 93 12:23:55 CDT
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: LIFE-X: cryonics

I have a question for those of you who are knowledgable about
cryonics.  Would the following idea be viable as a near-tern
activity?:

It has been mentioned several times that cardiac surgery is
done with lowering body temperature to near freezing.  Some animal
experiments have taken animals to a lower temperature and revived
them.  I would assume that things like tumors are equally slowed
as any other part of the body's metabolism on cooling.  So here's
the idea: Assume you are a cancer victim, and have been given
6 months to live.  You have a choice to undergo standard therapy,
radiation and chamicals; or a choice to be placed under long-term
hibernation, at a low but not frozen temperature.  If the metabolic
rate is slowed by twenty times, for example, then instead of six
months you would have ten years to live.  During that time, treatments
may improve enough to better your survival chances.

This builds on the existing cryosurgery experience, and there
are lots of cancer patients each year, some fraction of which
would gamble their last six months or whatever of conscious life,
which would be under pain and disability from the treatments, versus
a chance to live until a better treatment or cure becomes available.

My guess is that if you can get 50:1 metabolic slowing, then you
could find people who have, say a year to live, chance 20% of that
for 10 years of stasis, on the hope that 10 years later the treatments
would give them at least a year to live.

What amount of metabolic slowing do you get at various temperatures,
and what are the limits to staying at the lower temperatures?  What
is the longest time someone has been kept at various temps below
37C body temperature?

Dani Eder

