Date: Fri, 7 May 93 11:25:19 -0700
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: The Incredibly High Cost of High Tech

Ray Cromwell writes:

>   Do you think the costs are going to continually spiral upwards or
>will the curve taper off? That is, will the amount of capital needed,
>say for .3 micron not differ that much than the amount needed for
>a .2 micron plant? Perhaps, $10 billion represents the cut off point
>and going beyond won't take much more (especially if manufacturing
>technology gets cost reduced)

Part of the cost is sheer size (area, support facilities), part of it is
clean room requirements (Fab 9 in Albuquerque is Class 10 in areas where
humans are allowed (in "space suits") and Class 1 in critical masking
areas...."Class x" is a measure of airborne particle contamination), part
of it is the cost of capital equipment ($2 million per stepper, and many
are needed), and so forth.

Going from 0.6 micron to 0.4 micron will allow chip integration to reach 10
million transistors, speeds to reach 125 MHz (forget Alpha's "200 MHz"
clock, as DEC had to devote a huge fraction of the chip area and power just
to distributing the clock),  and performance to >200 SPECmarks. However,
this will all cost a lot of money.

Whether $10 billion is the top or not, I don't know. It depends on how many
chips are being sold (as I mentioned in my post in response to Nick Szabo,
the cost is dependent on volumes, learning curve, etc.)

>  $100 billion for a chip plant? The Superconducting SuperCollider
>only costs ~$12 billion and that's government. I don't buy that
>figure, care to give some evidence supporting it reaching that level?

Scenario: Intel or someone like Intel becomes the chief supplier of
processors for high definition television (a la the General
Instruments/Microsoft/Intel television deal, designed to head Japan's HDTV
off at the pass). That, and "cyberspace decks." (a combinations of Windows
NT, the 3DO, etc.) 100 million homes and office sites in the U.S. and
perhaps another 100 million worldwide each have one and are prepared to
spend roughly $400-700 for the CPU portion (about what users are now paying
for new 66 MHz 486s), all in 1993 dollars of course. This represents a
market of $80-140 billion, which could "justify" building a $10-30 billion
factory (split into many pieces, obviously, as companies like Intel already
do...what with factories in Santa Clara, Portland, Phoenix, Albuquerque,
Ireland, Israel, and soon to be in New York (HOT FLASH RUMOR: Intel may buy
several of IBM's fabs in New York)).

If the cyberspace world takes off the way many of us think it will, then
having $50-100 billion tied up in manufacturing plants seems plausble, if
staggering. As I've said so many times before, the key is self-financing!!

The "road to nanotech," to get back to themes of more direct interest to
this list, must essentially be self-financing, as the chip industry was
from 1955 onwards. The simple circuits of 1960 funded the R&D and factories
of 1965 which built the first commercial TTL gates, and those circuits paid
for the factories of 1970, and so on. (I've made these points before.)

What's getting the Japanese into trouble now is that they _borrowed_ the
money to build their DRAM plants and now the loans are due and
payable...but the profits aren't enough to cover them! (This is a story
someone needs to write up for a major magazine.)

Exciting times.

-Tim
