Nick Szabo writes:

>Tim May:
>> That is, Intel will have about $5 billion tied up in wafer fabrication
>> facilities for the Pentium (0.6 micron process) as peak volume production
>> is reached, around 1996.
>
>This is an interesting figure.  A figure interesting wrt market entry 
>for new technologies is the cost of building the first plant.  What 
>is the minimum Intel could spend if, today, they decided that instead
>of increasing the market was going to be flat?  Copyright issues aside, 
>what is the minimum cost for Cyrix or AMD to get into the Pentium 
>business?  How does this compare to the entry costs for the 486 business?

A small 0.6 micron fab could be built for much, much less than the number I
cited, e.g., for $20 million or so. (The cost of one copy of each of the
critical pieces of equipment and a Class 10 cleanroom to hold them could be
even less, perhaps as little as $10 million. Some university labs can even
print 0.6 micron features--even smaller features as research projects.)

The problem with low-volume manufacturing is that key knowledge is never
learned, the learning curve is not traversed. A small factory will have a
hard time manufacturing devices with 3 million transistors on it.

"What about RISC?" I hear some say. The "SuperSPARC" from TI, aka "Viking,"
has had manufacturing problems for two years now. TI just can't seem to
make these big, complex chips. The line between RISC and CISC has
essentially vanished, as the ostensibly CISC chip, the Pentium (Pentacle,
for Eric Raymond), has superscalar execution and most instructions are
completed in one clock cycle (or less, with superscalar and other
pipelining techniques).

>Also interesting is the cost of chip fabs for SRAM,
>DRAM, and ASICs.   What scale are these fabbed on these days?
>Another interesting near-term nanotech-ish possibility
>is using STMs to read/write hard disk drives at an atomic scale, recently 
>announced in Japan (but currently the data rate is far too low).
>It might be possible to diversify nanotech R&D over several industries.

A memory shortage is developing, again. DRAM prices are climbing. Wise
Extropians might want to look into the "memory futures" market (which I
have heard still exists). The large Japanese companies bet heavily on
memories and are now suffering from insufficient profits to repay the huge
loans they took out in the mid-80s to build their plants (part of the great
"U.S. is losing the chip war!" ballyhoo in the 1983-89 period).


>A fab might spend $20 million on near-term nanotech research with an
>overall cumulative chance of success (as defined below) of 10%, in
>areas such as STM arrays, STM alignment, quantum dots, nanoresists, guided
>ion-beam epitaxy, or other advanced submicron technologies.  "Success"
...rest of speculation deleted, as I have little to add to it....

One thing to realize is that companies cannot even think about depending on
some as-yet-undeveloped piece of equipment in a critical path. A
multibillion dollar fab cannot sit idle while it awaits a new invention.
Thus, any R&D on equipment should be done by *equipment vendors*. Then if
the idea is a good one (the wafer stepper being an example, from circa
1980), it will sell to many companies.


