Tom Morrow:
>The problem has never been access to adjudication.  Those caught in flame
>wars have always had the option of picking a third party to settle their
>disputes.  

I think the question is whether ExI will honor contracts made before a
dispute has arisen, about who should judge any disputes between two
parties.  This is best time to pick an neutral arbitrator.  "Honor"
means hold people to those contracts.  If they agreed to used judge A,
and then one side changes their mind once a dispute has arisen, the
List Administrator refuses to judge the case.

Harry continues:
>My only concern here is that one party (ExI) has a very strong
>interest in the outcome of any case, because they own the list. ...
>ExI could have a "test" that third party judges need to "pass" before 
>they offer their services. ...
>ExI could simply be given veto power over third party judges.

In practice I don't see much difference between ExI having veto power
over cases or judges, or them being able to bring separate charges in
a case.  The later seems conceptually simpler, so why not go with that?

Tom isn't sure we need to require the list administrator be notified
of disputes.  I now agree with him.  If one side is unhappy, and
thinks the LA might help them, they'll probably notify of their own
accord.

****  SOOOO..

I hereby officially propose that ExI honor private prior contracts
specifying who should judge future disputes regarding Extropy rules.

And I declare a list-specific trademark of the use of the phrase
"Preferred-Judges:" in a header or signature, followed by a list of
judges.  This phrase shall mean that the poster currently prefers
these judges, and contractually agrees to the following method of
choosing judges:

  Using the Preferred-Judges: lists of the two parties as of one week
  before the day that an accuser officially brings charges against an
  accused, the case shall be decided by the first judge on the 
  accused's list that also appears on the accuser's list, and who 
  agrees to judge the case.

If no judge fits this criteria, then of course the list administrator
is to judge the case.  Note that you don't need to get prior
permission from the judge, though simple politeness dictates that you
should delete any potential judge to asks you to do so.

This approach prefers the accused's preferred judge, because an
accusor might change their preferred judges, or choose an accused, in
order to gain an advantage.

