Perry Metzger writes:

>@Begin(pedagog-mode)
>
>Tim unfortunately is perpetuating a bad meme here -- the "Financing
>technique X is the best" meme. In fact, capitalization structure of
>large corporate ventures often depends on the prevailing interest
...rest elided to save space...

To be sure, I agree that there are many routes to financing. Milken was
unfairly demonized for creating a very useful set of financing instruments
(junk bonds, etc.). (I'd even like to see a free market in even wilder
financing schemes, such as easier sales of shares in highly speculative
ventures to the public, as we've discussed here before.)

Regarding debt, there's nothing intrinsically wrong with borrowing money to
start a venture if the venture looks like it'll succeed. But what I like
about the relatively more conservative approach of financing expansion out
of current sales is that it forces a company to build its future on current
market successes. The feedback loop is tighter.

In my opinion, the availability of a lot of capital for borrowing in the
mid-80s in Japan (all those pension funds and banks with tight links to
their group companies) allowed the major Japanese chip companies to all
"bet on the distant future" and build DRAM capacity. It looks like they
guessed wrong.

For nanotech, I do feel strongly that companies in that business need to
find profit-making products as quickly as possible. This will allow them to
produce products and move down the learning curve, gaining knowledge about
their products and pursuing opportunities as they arise. Also, few
investors will wait for the (probable, but I admit none of us knows) 10-20
years it may take before "real" nanotech products begin to arrive in
volume.

What investors in 1968 would've waited for the microprocessor to really
take off in the late 70s? (Fortunately, Intel became profitable via sales
of DRAMs, EPROMs, etc., in the early 1970s and was thereafter able to pay
off its investors, via stock appreciation, and finance its own growth.)

But I certainly appreciate Perry's analysis. I just wouldn't call my
comments "perpetuating a bad meme."


-Tim

